\par\par \b \uc2  Goldman trims forecast gains in South African rand.  \b0\par\par\uc2 298 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2000\par 11:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited \par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 12 (Reuters) - Goldman Sachs cut its forecasts for how much the South African rand will strengthen against the dollar over the next 12 months, according to its latest client note.  \par \par \uc2  The U.S. investment bank said it continued to recommend investors maintain short dollar positions against the rand but cut its forecasts to 6.7 rand in three months, 6.4 in six months and 6.7 in 12 months from its previous forecasts of 6.30, 6.25 and 6.40 respectively.  \par \par \uc2  The rand stood at 6.7975/8075 per dollar at 0950 GMT.  \par \par \uc2  The relatively peaceful outcome of elections in Zimbabwe and signs that South African inflation would shortly be peaking were seen as rand supportive.  \par \par \uc2  But Goldman said it expected rand strength to be capped by the likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserve Bank to reduce its forward book.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net open \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position (NOFP) - which measures its uncovered exposure to the forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - fell to $10.1 billion in June from $10.2 billion in May.  \par \par \uc2  Goldman Sachs expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the repo rate at 11.75 percent for the rest of this year and sees further easing in the second quarter of next year.  \par \par \uc2  It saw this coming amid a significant decline in inflation as the effect of higher oil prices and this year's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  correction dissipated and the positive benefits of structural reform dominated.  \par \par \uc2  The bank maintained its forecast of 12.8 percent yield on the R150 bond for the end of 2000 and 12 percent by mid-2001. It was yielding around 13.25 percent on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 20 7542 6176, andy.reierson@reuters.com)).  \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7c01yii\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
