\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  hit new highs, new lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 16:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 6 (Reuters) - The Israeli shekel and Slovak crown broke through new levels on Monday as investment flows pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ever higher while the rand hit an 18-month low against the dollar, echoing weakness in the local bond market. \par \par \uc2  The shekel broke through 4.0 to the dollar and was trading at 3.9997 bid at 1430 GMT, after hitting its highest levels since October 1998. \par \par \uc2  "It's heading for a very key level around 3.99 because the turning point between a possible bounce and a continuance downwards," said one trader. "If it breaks 3.99 it should continue to around 3.95." \par \par \uc2  He said the market was waiting to see what the Nasdaq would do after making strong gains on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There is a kind of umbilical cord between the two. If the Nasdaq does shoot above the 5,000 level we'll see the Shekel gain ground," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq opened unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown pushed ahead to its highest levels against the euro since the euro's birth, trading at 41.62 compared to Friday's close of 41.75, helped by positive sentiment on the country's outlook. \par \par \uc2  "We like the economy - there's an improving current account performance and lots of state asset sales in the pipeline. There are good reasons for the improvement in the crown," said Tim Ash, economist at WestLB. \par \par \uc2  Ash said the market was now trying to find a level after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 42.3 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  the rand was telling a different story, hitting lows against the dollar not seen for the last 18 months, at 6.48 compared to Friday's close of 6.45. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally there's nothing that's been driving this. News has been very encouraging," said Razia Khan at Standard Chartered. She said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lost some credibility last week when it said its inflation targeting policy had been a bit rushed, though it is still likely to meet the targets. \par \par \uc2  With no major data due out in the next week for South Africa and investors also shunning the bond market, there is little to change the current situation, Khan said. \par \par \uc2  "Until we see a real recovery in the euro it is unlikely the rand will strengthen much against the dollar. But if you take into account the movements against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of all South Africa's trading partners, in real terms the rand has been holding up strongly." \par \par \uc2  (Caroline Smith, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6414, caroline.smith@reuters.com). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3605cj7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
