\par\par \b \uc2  IMF praises as well as chides S.Africa - finmin. \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1998\par 17:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CAPE TOWN, Sept 9 (Reuters) - South African Finance Minister Trevor Manuel said on Wednesday an annual review of the country's economy by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) had praised as well as criticised financial policy. \par \par \uc2  Manuel told a news conference the Cabinet had noted the report issued last week, which he said was positive on the progress South Africa had made in controlling expenditure in its provinces. He said the IMF was also content with the country's tight monetary and fiscal policy and financial sector regulation. \par \par \uc2  "There were a number of headings where it was indicated that these issues were being dealt with adequately," he said. \par \par \uc2  The report was particularly encouraging on South Africa's performance in regulating the financial sector and banking supervision, which was particularly important in the wake of banking crises in South East Asia, Manuel said. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister admitted that the Washington-based agency had taken \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  to task over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forward market to support the rand earlier this year and inadequate labour market flexibility. \par \par \uc2  The IMF report said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been wrong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the rand and should in future raise interest rates instead. The agency said South Africa's "uneven monetary policy" coupled with buying the rand on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had expose the economy to greater risk. \par \par \uc2  The rand came under heavy selling pressure in May as other emerging market economies suffered devaluations in the wake of last year's financial crisis that engulfed Asian countries. \par \par \uc2  Official interest rates in South Africa have surged to 21.855 percent from 15 percent in April as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended the rand from speculators who have robbed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 25 percent of its value against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said South Africa should close all its forward market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  run by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , linked to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and urgently adopt a comprehensive policy response to restore market confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This should include keeping a tight monetary policy, refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , achieving its 1998/99 fiscal year budget targets and accelerating previously announced economic reforms, it said. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister said the document would become a basis of engagement at the forthcoming IMF annual meeting. \par \par \uc2  "You take these points, but it doesn't mean that there is an absolute correctness because it comes from the IMF," Manuel said. "We would reserve the right to take decisions as a sovereign member of the IMF and differ with them." \par \par \uc2  Manuel said Cabinet had also improved the draft Insider Trading Bill which aims to improve monitoring, investigation and penalties of irregular share dealing and said the legislation should be passed early next year. \par \par \uc2  ((-Emma Thomasson, Parliamentary newsroom +27 21 4032502 reutercp@iafrica.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9900pwq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoa