\par\par \b \uc2  S.Africa markets face rough ride after IMF critique. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Darren Schuettler \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1998\par 23:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, Sept 2 (Reuters) - South Africa's bruised financial markets faced another rough ride on Thursday after a damning report on the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  The Washington-based agency said on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been wrong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect its battered rand and should in future raise interest rates instead. \par \par \uc2  "I think the markets will take it quite badly tomorrow morning when they wake up to this IMF report," said Mike Schussler, an economist at FBC Merchant Bank in Johannesburg. \par \par \uc2  "It's very unfortunate that the IMF would come out with a statement criticising South Africa's policy when the general consumer is in danger of falling over." \par \par \uc2  South Africa's economy has been reeling from a four-month \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis that has seen interest rates raised to the highest levels in a decade, threatening growth and dashing the hopes of an improved life for millions of poor blacks. \par \par \uc2  The rand has shed 30 percent of its value against the dollar since late May as speculators targeted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid growing emerging market jitters. \par \par \uc2  The Johannesburg bourse has suffered a 42 percent collapse since April, taking stocks to levels not seen since the eve of South Africa's first all-race elections in 1994 when many feared a civil war. \par \par \uc2  The recent Russian financial crisis added to South Africa's woes last week, but Wednesday's session had seen stock and money markets begin to stabilise. \par \par \uc2  "That is a very bad report to have in these circumstances," another economist said in forecasting a tough day for domestic markets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rand eased to around 6.23 (bid) to the dollar in overnight trading from 6.21 (bid) when the local market closed at 1400 GMT on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In its review, the IMF said South Africa's "uneven monetary policy" and buying of the rand on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had exposed the economy to greater risk earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  "Directors expressed concern that, following an initial increase in interest rates, the authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot and forward exchange markets and the pursuit of an uneven monetary policy had exacerbated the pressures and may have risked converting a contagion problem into one that was more specific to South Africa," said the IMF report. \par \par \uc2  The IMF urged a comprehensive policy response to restore market confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including keeping a tight monetary policy, refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , achieving government budget targets and accelerating previously announced reforms. \par \par \uc2  "If renewed pressures develop, the Reserve Bank (of South Africa) would need to tighten monetary policy again," the IMF report said. \par \par \uc2  It also warned that reducing interest rates too hastily would encourage inflation and risk a further speculative attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In hindsight, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have raised interest rates earlier to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economists said, but they considered the IMF's criticism unfair. \par \par \uc2  "It's very easy to criticise the Reserve Bank, but you must remember at the time it was unclear that the contagion effect would go as far as it did," said John Reed of ABN-AMRO in Johannesburg. \par \par \uc2  There was no immediate comment from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Reserve Bank Governor Chris Stals said earlier on Wednesday that global market turmoil prevented a quick rate reduction. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing growing domestic pressure to ease monetary policy as the country teeters on the edge of a recession. \par \par \uc2  Official rates have soared to 21.855 percent from 15 percent in April, threatening homeowners and businesses. \par \par \uc2  Stals said South Africa had the stamina to weather the storm buffeting world markets, but the immediate future was beyond official control. \par \par \uc2  "Return of stability to these markets will pave the way for lower rates in South Africa...For any individual country such as South Africa to break out of these shackles at this stage will be almost impossible," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Johannesburg newsroom, 27 11 482 1003, newsroom@reuters.co.za)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du92021fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
