\par\par \b \uc2  Cenbank dlr/rand sales fail to impress-analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 20:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 26 (Reuters) - The South African rand on Friday plummeted to a record low, failing to grasp a lifeline extended in New York trade when central banks were seen helping Johannesburg bolster the battered rand. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said both the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the Bank of England sold the dollar against the rand on behalf of South Africa in mid-morning New York trading but later noted the efforts appeared fruitless. \par \par \uc2  Market players were surprised to see the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rand briefly stabilised on the news. But it soon resumed its sharp decline and hit a low of 5.9605 rand to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve Bank of New York, the Bank of England and the Reserve Bank of South Africa all declined to comment when asked whether and why they had sold dollar/rand. \par \par \uc2  New York dealers said they saw the Federal Reserve sell at 5.74 and again at 5.71 while the Bank of England was seen selling at 5.6950. The sales caught the market by surprise and briefly stabilised the South African unit. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers quickly pronounced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ineffective in stopping the rand's slide, possibly because markets realise South Africa has insufficient reserves to mount a longer-term rand defence. \par \par \uc2  "They (South African Reserve Bank) can't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and spend the world, because they don't have it. It's like the gun is empty and they've got to turn elsewhere for ammunition," said David Durrant, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at consulting group I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  Dealers could not estimate the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though some said the central banks had been buying rand in chunks of $20 million. \par \par \uc2  South Africa "hasn't got much in the way of reserves, so we don't know exactly how much they did. While they moved it back up, obviously there was nothing behind it, so in that sense (the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) was a disaster," added Nick Douch, corporate risk advisor at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  While Friday's move marked a dramatic shift for \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has previously preferred to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through commercial banks rather than foreign central banks, dealers said it was not enough. \par \par \uc2  "I think they probably asked the Fed and the Bank of England to help in order to make a statement, because anytime we see a big \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in there it makes people nervous, but it doesn't seem to have worked," a New York dealer said. \par \par \uc2  With markets clearly ready to pummel the rand lower still, dealers and analysts agreed there was no way to predict where the decline may end or even slow. \par \par \uc2  "It is impossible to give levels here, we have been gapping all over the place," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rand has come under increasing pressure since late May when speculators rounded on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a worldwide crisis in emerging markets left it an easy target. The slump also came in spite of a firm increase in official interest rates Friday. \par \par \uc2  While South Africa weathered last summer's Asia crisis well, analysts were now bracing for more serious problems and agreed one way to help the rand more would be to express international concern. \par \par \uc2  "The only way you could get around this is if nations like the U.S. and the UK are genuinely worried and they would come out and say we are concerned with the way markets are treating these emerging markets and we are therefore \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  ourselves," Douch said. \par \par \uc2  But he and others said "there is very little chance that this would happen." \par \par \uc2  ((-NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6q0202a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
