\par\par \b \uc2  S. Africa rand in freefall despite Fed report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alister Bull \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1998\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, South Africa (Reuters) - The central banks of Britain and the United States rode to the rescue of South Africa's collapsing rand on Friday, dealers said, but the unprecedented support failed to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  Reports from New York dealers that the Federal Reserve and Bank of England had bought rand for dollars shocked analysts, who reckon that it was the first time that South Africa had enlisted U.S. and British help to boost its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the news failed to quell a savage speculative assault which has inflicted losses of over 8 percent against the dollar in just 24 hours. \par \par \uc2  Both the Federal Reserve and officials at the Reserve Bank of South Africa declined to confirm whether there had been a round of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of England also declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The rand briefly hesitated when the reports were published - and then resumed its headlong slide. \par \par \uc2  Analysts fear that foreign sellers have 6.00 rand to the dollar in their sights and warned it was futile to damage the weak domestic economy by using interest rates to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against an all-out assault. \par \par \uc2  "If they fire the Fed and the BOE off and they still can't hold back speculators then God help everyone else," said John Clemmow, Africa analyst at Investec Securities in London. \par \par \uc2  The rand's losses rode roughshod over a firm increase in official interest rates earlier on Friday, which initially lent support to the country's battered bond and stock markets before fresh waves of rand selling put them back on the ropes. \par \par \uc2  Rand value against the dollar has been slashed from 5.080 since late May to a new low of 5.92 - or a fall of over 15 percent - as speculators rounded on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a worldwide crisis in emerging markets left it an easy target. \par \par \uc2  Economists thought that the Bank had opted to let the market have its way, rather than waste hard-won foreign reserves in a futile bid to battle speculators. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to support the rand in May - employing 26 billion rand ($4.58 billion) to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the early stages of an emerging market assault. \par \par \uc2  Most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taken through its forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  book and the amount represents a significant share of its gross official reserves, which stood at 32.9 billion rand ($5.8 billion) at the end of May. \par \par \uc2  The Bank has since signalled it would revert to its well-worn strategy of "leaning against the wind" - only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the rand's descent. \par \par \uc2  But analysts wondered whether the Bank had decided to have another go at the speculators but lacked the fire-power needed. \par \par \uc2  "South African authorities don't have the means to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, they don't have enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said economist Magan Mistry at Nedcor in Johannesburg. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's alternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be to allow interest rates to rise in the rand's defence, but a hike in official repo rates to 18.31 percent from 17.34 percent earlier on Friday failed to turn the tide. \par \par \uc2  Rates had soared to a 13-year high of almost 24 percent on Monday but had been allowed to relax over the week as a semblance of stability returned. \par \par \uc2  The run on the rand shattered any illusion that South Africa's economy was out of the woods. \par \par \uc2  Economists argue that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should allow the rand to slip rather than chance a prolonged period of high interest rates, which would stifle essential growth and even topple the fragile economy into full-blown recession. \par \par \uc2  The stock market closed down but above session lows after a modest afternoon recovery pared losses to 61.7 points at 6,874.3. Bonds were spectators to the afternoon's gyrations, with most accounts happy to stick to the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark R150s, due 2005-2007, closed at a yield of 14.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6q01zwq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
