\par\par \b \uc2  SAfrica stocks to keep wary eye on rand and mkts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Darren Schuettler \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 1998\par 13:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, May 29 (Reuters) - South African stocks, emerging from their worst week since January, will keep a wary eye on a wobbly rand and volatile world markets next week, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Despite a two percent rebound for Johannesburg shares on Friday, they said several risks were lurking in the wings for South African equities. \par \par \uc2  "In the short term I still think there is further weakness coming, particularly into next week," said Murray Winckler, an equity strategist with Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Johannesburg. \par \par \uc2  "We are now factoring in a higher risk premium into our markets and we believe in the next three to six months equity markets will reach new highs," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Johannesburg's key All Share Index surrendered over six percent this week, including a five percent drop on Wednesday, as investors fled in the face of market turmoil at home and abroad - particularly in Russia and Asia. \par \par \uc2  The rand sank to record lows this week, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Reserve Bank to hike interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets to prop up the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But in equities, bargain hunters sparked a recovery on Friday, pushing the All Share Index up 2.2 percent by the close of trading. \par \par \uc2  Financial stocks, which bore the brunt of this week's selling, led the rebound with a 4.6 percent rally on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The sector suffered heavy losses this week amid fears that higher rates would narrow bank margins and trim profits. \par \par \uc2  South Africa's major banks held their prime lending rates in check, but analysts did not rule out a move next week to follow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nearly two percentage-point rate hike on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "There still seems to be a fairly even chance the banks could jack their prime rates up next week," Winckler said. \par \par \uc2  Equity traders will also keep an eye on next week's economic data, including gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Local and offshore traders have speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cash reserves have been drained by up to $2 billion dollars after its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the month. \par \par \uc2  However, some analysts said the reserves were likely to have fallen by only half that amount. \par \par \uc2  Richard Jesse, a director at Fleming Martin, said investors will have few corporate results to watch next week and will likely turn their attention to other world markets. \par \par \uc2  The only major earnings reports next week are conglomerate Tongaat-Hulett  on Monday and mining financial giant Anglo American Corp of South Africa Ltd  on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "You tend to find in June that results start to peter out. The market will probably occupy itself by looking closely at other world emerging markets and the U.S. market," Jesse said. \par \par \uc2  Firmer European stocks on Friday after Wall Street's higher close have eased market fears over emerging markets in Asia and Russia, which suffered heavy losses this week. \par \par \uc2  "We should have a less volatile week than the one we've just had. It has been a rather abnormal week," Jesse said. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts said this week they expect South African stocks to stay in the grip of market bears for several months before the trend turns upward again. \par \par \uc2  They said a pullback was long overdue for a market that has soared 30 percent in the last four months. The All Share Index could work a range between 6,500 and 7,000 in the months ahead before resuming its climb, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The overall picture is more consolidation and pullbacks during the next four to five months and also more volatility," said technical analyst Victor Hugo. \par \par \uc2  "Generally negative sentiment until Asia and the emerging market picture stabilises," he added. \par \par \uc2  ((Johannesburg newsroom, 27 11 482 1003, \par \par \uc2  newsroom@reuters.co.za)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5t048q7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
