\par\par \b \uc2  IMF urges forceful South African economic reforms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adam Entous \par \uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Aug 25 (Reuter) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) warned South Africa on Monday its economy is vulnerable to the whims of foreign investors and urged it to press ahead with economic reforms for its own good and that of the region. \par \par \uc2  In a statement released after a recent review of the country's economy, the IMF board called on Pretoria to speed up its privatization program, cut trade tariffs, keep interest rates high and maintain a tight grip on government finances. \par \par \uc2  "Directors encouraged the authorities to forcefully implement their stabilization and adjustment strategy to help create a prosperous South Africa, which would not only benefit the people of South Africa but also the regional economy," the IMF said, summing up the board's discussion on July 11. \par \par \uc2  The IMF regularly reviews the economies of all its member nations. Subject to the agreement of the country, it then releases a summary of the talks. \par \par \uc2  The board welcomed South Africa's determination to reduce its budget deficit, but several directors cautioned that a recent wage agreement with civil servants might make that difficult. \par \par \uc2  The IMF also urged the country to take "bold initiatives" to increase job opportunities for unskilled workers through more flexible wage rates and stepped-up training. \par \par \uc2  But several IMF board directors cautioned the country against plans to levy a payroll tax to pay for increased training programs, arguing that might hinder job creation. \par \par \uc2  The board also took issue with \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rand. \par \par \uc2  It argued that heavy purchases of the rand created confusion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and left the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  open to the risk of big trading losses. \par \par \uc2  The rand last week hit a record-low against the U.S. dollar before bouncing back, in part because of determined buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to protect the rand, the IMF advised the South African authorities to tighten financial policies. \par \par \uc2  A few directors even recommended that Pretoria increase interest rates if the rand faces renewed heavy selling. \par \par \uc2  The board generally agreed that interest rates should not be cut, as the growth of money supply and credit remains well above target. \par \par \uc2  "Directors generally cautioned against an early reduction in the Bank rate," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8p00tl4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
