\par\par \b \uc2  IMF warns S. Africa against big  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Aug 25 (Reuter) - The International Monetary Fund warned \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday against heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead urged it to carry out tough economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  In a statement released after a review of the country's economy, the IMF board argued that heavy purchases of the rand created confusion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and left the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  open to the risk of big trading losses. \par \par \uc2  "(The board) cautioned against large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," the IMF said, summarizing a discussion that took place on July 11. \par \par \uc2  The rand last week hit a record-low against the U.S. dollar, before bouncing back, in part because of determined buying by South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was under pressure for most of 1996, in what the IMF described as a "stark change in investor sentiment" about South Africa's future. To keep the rand from falling further, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, building large forward position. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said its board of directors was concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's big net open forward position and stressed the importance of reducing it "on a sustainable basis." \par \par \uc2  "Further upward pressures (on the rand) should be used to reduce (the forward position)," the IMF said. "Substantial downward pressures on the rand should be resisted through a tightening of financial policies," it added. \par \par \uc2  A few directors even advised Pretoria to increase interest rates if the rand faced renewed heavy selling. \par \par \uc2  The board generally agreed that interest rates should not be cut, as the growth of money supply and credit remains well above target. \par \par \uc2  "Directors generally cautioned against an early reduction in the Bank rate," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  The board urged South Africa to press ahead with tough economic reforms, saying that would help not only the country but the region as well. \par \par \uc2  It specifically called on Pretoria to speed up its privatization program and to use the money to pay down its debt. It also urged wide-ranging cuts in trade tariffs. \par \par \uc2  The board welcomed the country's determination to reduce its budget deficit, but several directors fretted that a recent wage agreement with civil servants might make that difficult. \par \par \uc2  It also called on the country to take "bold initiatives" to increase job opportunities for unskilled workers through more flexible wage rates and stepped-up training. \par \par \uc2  Several directors though cautioned the country against plans to levy a payroll tax to pay for increased training programs, arguing that might hinder job creation. \par \par \uc2  "Directors encouraged the authorities to forcefully implement their stabilization and adjustment strategy to help create a prosperous South Africa, which would not only benefit the people of South Africa but also the regional economy," the IMF said, summing up the board's discussion. \par \par \uc2  -- Washington newsroom, ((202-898-8310)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters L