\par\par \b \uc2  S.African rand moves into uncharted territory. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Richardson \par \uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 1996\par 15:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, Aug 1 (Reuter) - South Africa's embattled rand continued its plunge on Thursday, hitting new lows against the British pound and the German mark. \par \par \uc2  In early trade, the rand hit 4.53 against the dollar -- a level last seen in the eye of the rand storm three months ago. \par \par \uc2  The all-time low against sterling was 7.0455 rand while against the mark it slid to 3.0816 rand. \par \par \uc2  The renewed rand slide, which economists believe is unwarranted in terms of South Africa's economic fundamentals, has been blamed on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. \par \par \uc2  Technical charts, predicting future price moves from past developments, also paint a gloomy picture. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a demand for dollars both from locally and offshore because of the sentiment at the moment," said Willie Potgieter, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Standard Bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders say sentiment is being driven by factors including: \par \par \uc2  - labour opposition to government's macro-economic strategy; \par \par \uc2  - recurring rumours that Reserve Bank Governor Chris Stals plans to resign despite his denials, repeated again this week; \par \par \uc2  - a recent batch of poor data on money supply, trade balance and inflation; \par \par \uc2  - and slow progress on privatisation and the removal of exchange controls. \par \par \uc2  President Nelson Mandela echoed economists' views that there was no direct reason for the rand's fall and also moved to quell the rumours about Stals, saying that he thought Stals was "the best man for the job". \par \par \uc2  "I am also convinced from what he (Stals) has told me that he is prepared to serve for as long as we want him. I have no reason to doubt his sincerity in giving me that statement," Mandela told reporters in Pretoria on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Stals, appointed in the apartheid era, is seen as a strong supporter of cautious monetary policy designed to keep inflation in check. \par \par \uc2  Edward Osborn, consultant to stockbroker Edey, Rogers (Pty) Ltd, felt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness stemmed from a general lack of confidence in South Africa which had not been improved by government's unveiling of its macro-economic strategy in June. \par \par \uc2  "As we are being seen abroad, this is not a dynamic economy at the moment. It's pretty flat and full of political problems," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has little in its power to stem the exodus from the rand, having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in the past five months only to see its holding of gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dwindle to about one month's import cover. \par \par \uc2  Board of Executors economic consultant Rob Lee said the bank's limited ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favour of the rand meant \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now dependent on net inflows of capital on a virtually daily basis. \par \par \uc2  Osborn said current events on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market mirrored what happened in 1995 when the rand was propped up by the apparent strength of the capital account, which was entirely attributable to portfolio investments and mounting short-term indebtedness. \par \par \uc2  "We have had a reversal of that which has exposed the overall weakness of the capital account," Osborn said. \par \par \uc2  "Being so weak on the capital account now, we are that much more vulnerable to international speculation." \par \par \uc2  The rand has lost more than a fifth of its value since mid-February, in a sell-off triggered initially by ill-founded rumours about President Nelson Mandela's health and talk of the imminent scrapping of exchange controls. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds8104wmz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
