\par\par \b \uc2  Rand's fall is early warning to cool euphoria. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Soderlund \par \uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 1996\par 14:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, Feb 21 (Reuter) - The collapse of the rand has raised fears of a repeat of 1995's Mexican financial crisis, but economists said on Wednesday there were important differences which should prevent South African markets spiralling out of control. \par \par \uc2  Dave Mohr, chief economist at assurer Old Mutual, said the rand fall was an early warning to government and investors, who may have been gripped by unsustainable euphoria recently. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe we were getting onto a Mexican wave, and we've had at least an early warning from the markets that everything is not hunky-dory with economic policy," he said. "But I don't think it's as serious as Mexico." \par \par \uc2  The rand has fallen nearly 10 percent and bonds 12 percent since last Thursday, hit by unfounded rumours about President Nelson Mandela's health and talk of an imminent easing in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. \par \par \uc2  The plunge of the Mexican peso, which led to sharp falls in share and bond prices in emerging markets, was exacerbated by fruitless \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In contrast \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's central Reserve Bank appeared to be adopting a relatively hands-off approach, despite sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smoothe market fluctuations, analysts said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pummelling at the hands of the international capital markets was the latest example of how vulnerable developing economies can be to the sentiments of foreign investors, analysts said \par \par \uc2  "It looks like a bit of a confidence crisis which can turn around as quickly as it developed," said Tony Twine of Econometrix. But he cautioned that "anything is possible". \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a growing consensus among foreigners that the rand was overvalued had fuelled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  The same foreign investors had been largely responsible for the strength of the rand by pouring money into local bonds and stocks during 1995, noted Edward Osborn, consultant at Edey, Rogers & Co. \par \par \uc2  "They are getting their money out and pulling the rand down all the time," he said. \par \par \uc2  Another analyst said some foreign investors were running scared. "The guys got badly burnt in Mexico...Some foreigners lost 30 or 40 percent of their money overnight...everyone's just panicking because we don't have a lot of reserves," he said. \par \par \uc2  South Africa's reserves stood at 15.5 billion ($3.9 billion) in January -- levels which the Bank has said it would be attempting to bolster before exchange controls were relaxed any further. The country also has standby reserves at the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Reserve Bank's avowed policy of being present only to "soothe" the market's fluctuations would ensure the unit settled at a level which was more sustainable. \par \par \uc2  "The reserves are certainly totally inadequate to mount any sort of defence...It's spitting into the wind," said Osborn. \par \par \uc2  ($1=3.965 rand) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2l0183d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
